N2  -  Purpose/Objective(s): On‐treatment evaluation of patients undergoing radiation therapy (RT) and chemoradiation (CRT) is important for managing symptoms related to disease, RT, or systemic therapy. Automated extraction of clinical symptoms from free text documentation can enable the implementation of machine learning (ML) or artificial intelligence (AI) tools such as our previously developed pre‐treatment ML algorithm to predict ED visits and hospitalization during treatment. We present preliminary analysis of extracting on‐treatment symptom data from clinical notes via a natural language processing (NLP) pipeline. Materials/Methods: We obtained free‐text note data for 6,918 outpatient RT or CRT courses for adult patients at Duke from 2013‐2016. A natural language processing system default clinical pipeline was used to extract SNOMED terms representing the stated presence or absence of toxicities from on‐treatment evaluation notes. These were converted to CTCAE terms (possible 836 total) via the Observational Health Data Sciences and Informatics (OHDSI) Athena vocabulary. The performance was evaluated in 95 randomly selected notes in comparison to gold standard manual abstraction of CTCAE toxicities by a radiation oncologist in training. Detected symptoms with both positive and negative mentions were considered positive. Sensitivity and specificity were calculated on a per symptom basis as well as in aggregate across all notes and symptoms. Results: By human review, 54 symptom terms were identified as present and 35 were identified as negated in the reviewed notes; 24 present (44.4%) symptoms and 19 negated (54.3%) symptoms did not have direct mapping in Athena. Most commonly present symptoms were radiation dermatitis (23.2%), fatigue (16.8%), mucositis (10.5%), nausea (9.5%), cystitis (9.5%), and pain (8.4%). NLP had good performance for detecting fatigue (sensitivity 62.5%; specificity 83.5%), nausea (88.9%; 95.3%), and pain (75%; 69%). Detection of non‐infectious cystitis was poor despite mapping (0%; 100%). Most common negated terms were radiation dermatitis (32.6%), seroma (17.9%), chest wall pain (16.8%), colitis (15.8%), and diarrhea (15.8%). Sensitivity was poor for negated symptoms, with best performance in detecting negation of fatigue (36.3%; 94.0%), dysphagia (62.5%; 98.9%), and pruritis (71.4%; 93.1%). Among symptoms with available mapping, NLP had aggregate sensitivity of 61.0% and specificity of 97.4% of identifying present symptoms. For negation of symptoms, aggregate sensitivity was 19.8% and specificity was 98.9%. Conclusion: Natural language processing can provide fair identification of toxicity during therapy, but appropriate vocabulary was limited in our current workflow. Sensitivity is poor for the stated absence of symptoms. Ongoing and future work focuses on improving CTCAE term mapping, additional human reviewers and evaluation of clinical notes, and incorporation into computational workflows.
