N2  -  The objective of this study was to determine the accuracy of Natural Language Processing (NLP) for identifying spondyloarthr‐opathy in lumbar spine imaging reports. Design: Diagnostic accuracy study Setting: Primary care clinics from four large integrated health systems. Participants: Primary care patients with lumbar spine imaging reports. Interventions: This research was part of the Lumbar Imaging with Reporting of Epidemiology (LIRE) study, a multisite randomized trial of providing epidemiologic information in lumbar spine imaging reports (x‐ray, MRI, CT). A set of lumbar spine imaging reports was enriched for spondyloarthropathy diagnoses using ICD‐9/10 diagnostic codes. The reference standard was the consensus clinician impression of the presence of spondyloarthropathy in the report, whereby two clinicians evaluated each report for spondyloarthropathy, with discrepancies resolved through discussion and final adjudication by a neuroradiologist where necessary. We examined the diagnostic accuracy of an NLP system implemented in Java, with open‐source software for negation detection, as compared to the clinician impression reference standard. Main Outcome Measures: Sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, negative predictive value of NLP for the identification of spondyloarthropathy in imaging reports, and 95% confidence intervals for these performance characteristics. Results: In the case‐enriched annotated sample of imaging reports (n=2594), the prevalence of spondyloarthropathy was 12%. The performance characteristics of NLP for the identification of spondy‐loarthropathy were: sensitivity = 95% (95% CI: 92‐97%), specificity = 99% (95% CI: 99‐99.9%), positive predictive value = 96% (95% CI: 93‐98%), negative predictive value = 99% (95% CI: 99‐99.9%). Conclusions: The diagnostic accuracy of NLP for identifying spondy‐loarthropathy on imaging reports appears to be high. These performance characteristics reflect the case‐enriched sample and may not be generalizable to the general population. Nevertheless, these findings suggest that NLP may be a useful tool for identifying rare imaging findings in large datasets of imaging reports, with potential applications for research or for clinical care.
