N2  -  Background and Objectives: Mortality prediction models are broadly used in research and quality improvement to adjust for severity of illness and can also inform triage decisions. The Mortality Probability Model (MPM) is the most relevant for emergency critical care (ECC) because it is calculated at the time of intensive care unit (ICU) admission. However, a limitation for its automated use or application to large data sets is that it includes the variable “intracranial mass effect” (IME), which requires human engagement with the electronic health record (EHR). We developed and tested a natural language processing (NLP) algorithm to identify IME from computed tomography (CT) head reports. We hypothesized that this algorithm can interpret free text CT reports to identify intracranial mass effect as well as a human reviewer. Methods: 1557 CT head reports from patients admitted to the ICU from our emergency department (ED) were labeled yes/no IME by two of five independent labelers. The reports were then randomly divided into a 1202‐record training set and a 355‐record test‐set. All reports were preprocessed to remove linguistic and style variability, and a dictionary was created to map similar common terms. We tested three vectorization strategies: Term Frequency‐Inverse Document frequency (TF‐IDF), Word2Vec, and Universal Sentence Encoder to convert the report text to a numerical vector for the machine learning model. After training, model performance was assessed in the test set using the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC). We also divided the continuous range of scores into yes/maybe/no categories for IME. Results: Of the 1202 CT reports in the training set, 308 (25.6%) reports were manually labelled as positive for IME. Of the 355 reports in the test set, 108 (30.4%) were labeled as positive for IME. The TF‐IDF model had the best AUROC:‐‐ 0.9625 (95% CI 0.9443‐0.9807). TF‐IDF score categories were defined and had the following likelihood ratios: yes >.5 (24.59); maybe .05‐0.5 (0.99), and no < 0.05 (0.05). 82% of reports were classified as either “Yes IME” or “No IME”. In the test set, only 4 of 199 (2.0%) reports with a “No IME” classification were false negatives and only 8 of 93 (8.6%) reports classified as “Yes IME” were false positives. Conclusion: NLP can accurately identify IME from free‐text reports of head CTs in a majority of cases, allowing automatic calculation of MPM based on EHR data.
