N2  -  Background Administrative codes have limited accuracy in identifying hospitalizations for worsening heart failure (WHF). We used natural language processing (NLP) for electronic health records to systematically detect primary admissions for WHF. Methods We randomly selected 50 hospitalizations with a primary discharge code for HF (derivation) and 75 hospitalizations with an equal mix of primary and secondary discharge codes for HF (validation) in a large, integrated health care delivery system. WHF was defined as ≥1 symptom, ≥2 objective findings including ≥1 sign, AND ≥1 change in HF‐related therapy. Vitals, labs, and medications were accessed. Provider notes and radiology reports were analyzed using I2E software (Linguamatics, v5.4.1). Manual chart review was performed by two physicians with final adjudication by a third. Results Among 75 patients in the validation cohort, mean age was 76±13 years, with 44% women and 31% with an ejection fraction <40%. The NLP algorithm had excellent performance compared to manual adjudication (Table). Ten (26%) hospitalizations with a secondary diagnostic code for HF met the definition of WHF based on manual review and were correctly detected by the NLP algorithm with only one additional false positive. Conclusion Systematic detection of WHF using an NLP algorithm is feasible and highly accurate. Our NLP algorithm could be widely applied for clinical practice and research including the identification of potentially unrecognized episodes of inpatient WHF. Performance of the NLP algorithm to classify manually adjudicated hospitalizations for WHF. [Figure presented]
