DO  -  10.1007/s11136-017-1658-6
N2  -  Aims: To apply trigrams (a Natural Language Processing (NLP) tool measuring frequency of three words reported sequentially together) to analyze verbatim patient‐reported outcomes (PROs) among Huntington Disease (HD) research participants in the Phase 2 REACH2HD randomized‐ controlled trial examining safety and cognitive benefits of PBT2, an experimental modulator of metal ionophores. Methods: The Huntington Disease Patient‐Reported Outcome of Problems (HDPROP) captures verbatim reports of patients' most bothersome problems due to neurological disease. The HD‐PROP was administered at baseline (BL) and 26 weeks (W26) of the REACH2HD trial to the 109 research participants, randomly assigned 1:1:1 to PBT2 250 mg/day, PBT2 100 mg/day, or placebo. Using NLP to extract relationships and meaning from large text‐based resources, trigrams of verbatim bothersome problems were analyzed at BL and W26 for each of the three treatment groups. Results: Within placebo and the 100mg PBT2 group at both BL and W26, “being able to” and “not being able” were most frequently reported trigrams. Within 250 mgPBT2 group atBL, the most frequently reported trigrams were also “being able to” (n = 7) and “not being able” (n = 6). However, at W26 the following five trigrams: “i used to”, “used to do”, “to do things”, “I have to”, and “I can't do” were all reported with 3 counts. Conclusions: NLP quantifies patterns in the verbatim HDPROP dataset of the REACH2HD trial. The trigram is clinically more meaningful compared to single word counts because it reflects patterns of human speech. Most commonly reported trigrams “being able to” and “not being able” demonstrate that PROs in this dataset prioritize both ability and inability to do tasks and provide examples of how clinical trial patients verbalize decline or improvement in functioning. The more wide‐ranging responses reported atW26 among the PBT2 250mg group suggests greater linguistic diversity, possibly related to cognitive function improvement from PBT2 treatment. NLP can be informatively applied to HD verbatim datasets; more advanced NLP methods could further refine PROs analysis in HD and other neurological disorders.
