N2  -  Background: Statistical models have been developed in the last two decades to predict clinical outcomes for patients hospitalized with heart failure. These predictive models were developed based on demographic, clinical, and laboratory data to stratify the risk of these patients. However, these models are not used routinely in clinical practice due to their complexity and low validity. To improve and simplify the prediction of the clinical outcomes of patients with heart failure, we developed deep learning models based only on the text of echocardiogram (echo)reports. Methods: Patients admitted with heart failure to the intensive care unit at Beth Israel Medical Center from 2001 to 2012 and who underwent an echocardiogram were included in this study. Patients were randomly divided into two groups: a training group (80% of all patients), used to develop artificial neural networks models, and a testing group (20% of all patients), used to evaluate the accuracy of these models. Natural Language Toolkit was used to preprocess the text of echocardiogram reports, and the artificial neural network models were programmed and trained in Python 3.7 to predict inpatient mortality and hospital readmissions within 30 days. Results: A total of 8,995 hospital admissions were included in this study. Of these admissions, there were 1,307 inpatient deaths and 694 hospital readmissions. Using echo report text, the accuracy of artificial neural network models for predicting 30‐days hospital readmissions and inpatient mortality were 92% and 85%, respectively. Conclusion: Artificial neural networks models based on echocardiogram reports provide an accurate, reliable and accessible method to predict inpatient mortality and hospital readmissions for patients admitted with heart failure. The models facilitate risk stratification of hospitalized heart failure patients and potentially guide management.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02086291/full
