N2  -  Introduction: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common inherited cardiomyopathy and the leading cause of sudden cardiac death in young adults. Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is the cornerstone for clinical diagnosis and management of HCM patients. Interpretations of MRI results are documented in radiology reports in electronic health records (EHR). The purpose of this study was to autonomously classify MRI narrative reports containing positive diagnoses of HCM using natural language processing (NLP) techniques. Methods: An observational cohort was retrieved from the Mayo Clinic enterprise data warehouse consisting of HCM patients (n=2,051) that had underwent any MRI from 1998 to 2018. Of these, 200 patients were randomly selected and allocated to training and test datasets (n=100 each). The training set contained 185 MRI reports, whereas the test set included 206 reports. 2 abstractors independently reviewed these MRI reports to determine whether they contained a positive diagnosis of HCM by following a written abstraction guideline. Discrepancies between these two abstractors were resolved via a third independent abstractor that acted as a tie‐breaker. Abstraction results served as a clinical reference standard. A rule‐based NLP algorithm using MedTagger software was developed using the training set which was then evaluated against the test set. The evaluation performance was determined via comparison of each MRI report's classification as determined by the NLP algorithm (contains/does not contain HCM diagnosis) against the clinical reference standard.
