N2  -  Objective: This study aims to apply the Natural Language Processing (NLP) tools of word clouds (visual single‐word count frequency depiction) and tri‐grams (frequency of three words reported sequentially together) to analyze verbatim consequence of patient‐reported outcomes (PROs) among Huntington disease (HD) research participants in the phase 2 REACH2HD randomized‐controlled trial examining safety and cognitive benefits of PBT2. Background(s): FDA and other regulatory agencies value PROs, but analytic approaches are lacking. The Huntington Disease Patient‐Reported Outcome of Problems (HD‐PROP) was developed to capture verbatim reports of patients' most bothersome problems and functional consequences due to HD. The HD‐PROP was administered at baseline (BL) and 26 weeks (W26) of the REACH2HD trial to the 109 research participants (randomly assigned 1:1:1 to PBT2 250 mg/day, PBT2 100 mg/day, or placebo). Method(s): Using NLP to extract relationships and meaning, word clouds and tri‐grams of verbatim consequences associated with verbatim bothersome problems were analyzed at BL and W26 for each treatment group. Result(s): Across all time and treatment groups, consequences word clouds displayed Bthings 111 counts, Bget 68 counts, and Blike 42 counts. The most frequently reported tri‐grams at baseline for all three groups reported decline from prior ability, such as Bit takes me longer and BI used to. At week 26, all three groups continued to report decline in prior function. Conclusion(s): NLP can quantify functional patterns in the REACH2HD trial verbatim HD‐PROP dataset. The tri‐gram is clinically more meaningful compared with a single word count because it provides context for consequences reported.Most commonly reported tri‐grams demonstrated that PROs in this dataset prioritize both ability and temporal changes in functioning and offer examples of how clinical trial patients verbalize accommodation, decline, or improvement in functioning associated with HD. More advanced NLP methods could further refine analysis of PROs in HD and other movement disorders.
