DO  -  10.1016/j.joca.2024.03.089
N2  -  Purpose (the aim of the study): Rehabilitation is essential to optimize outcomes after joint replacement surgeries which are commonly performed in advanced stages of osteoarthritis, such as in the knee or shoulder. However, low adherence to rehabilitation is one of the main barriers to maximizing recovery and return to activities. To address this public health issue, a novel strategy based on a natural language processing (NLP) tool was designed to promote adherence to rehabilitation and improve clinical outcomes. The aim was to determine the effectiveness of the proposal in promoting adherence to home rehabilitation after major orthopedic surgeries Methods: Two independent randomized clinical trials (RCT), with participants undergoing total knee arthroplasty (RCT1‐TKA) or reverse shoulder arthroplasty (RCT2‐RSA), were conducted in two Hospitals in Valencia (Spain) between 2020 and December 2023. The participants received postsurgical postoperative rehabilitation; the use of an NLP tool based on a chatbot (Figure 1 shows the operation flow) to assist with home exercises underpinned the difference between the experimental and control interventions. The participants were instructed with NLP software that communicated on personal smartphones with an automated chatbot. The tool was designed to inform on basic aspects such as the length and phases of the treatment. The tool was programmed to send automated messages every training day with recommendations, videos, and GIFs on how to exercise. The progress was reported at the end of each week, informing them about the degree of compliance achieved, and motivating them to continue exercising. Adherence to rehabilitation (considered as achieved when compliance rate was over 80%) and clinical measures of joint function (WOMAC, Quick‐DASH) and pain (VAS) were compared with CI set at 95%. All participants signed a consent form (ethics approval numbers 2020/350, 2021‐001‐1, and 2021‐931‐1) and there was a prospective publication of methods. Results: Of the 112 randomized patients, 65 with TKR and 34 with RSA completed the interventions. The tool was effective in increasing adherence to rehabilitation [RCT1: 89% vs. 74%; OR95%= 4.1 (1.2 to 14.9); RCT2: 77% vs. 65%; OR95%=8.1 (1.6 to 40.7; p=0.005)]. Participants in the experimental group presented benefits on self‐reported functionality (WOMAC, Quick‐DASH) and increased levels of physical activity (IPAQ‐E) with p<0.001 over standard care. However, pain levels were similar among groups after the interventions, regardless of the type of surgery Conclusions: A strategy to promote adherence to rehabilitation based on an NLP tool promoted adherence with early postoperative home rehabilitation in patients undergoing major orthopedic surgeries of knee and shoulder joint replacement due to advanced osteoarthritis condition. This translated into increased functional benefits, although no increased benefits on pain levels. Future trials can be oriented to determine the long‐term effectiveness of the proposal Disclosures: Funded by AEI, Spain; Project PID2020‐115825RA‐I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033. The funder played no role in the design and conduct of the study. The authors have no conflict of interest to declare [Formula presented] NLP tool operation flow
