N2  -  Rationale. Sepsis is the most common and costly cause of hospitalization in the US and kills 6 million people annually worldwide. We sought to use comprehensive electronic medical record (EMR) data to identify distinct clinical signatures among infection and sepsis patients that could help inform future targeted treatment approaches. Methods. We randomly selected 30,000 of 167,833 suspected infection and sepsis encounters admitted directly from the emergency department between 2010 and 2013 at 21 hospitals in an integrated health system in Northern California. Using EMR data, we extracted all medications, procedures, and orders during each patient's first 24 hours of hospitalization, removing elements that occurred only once overall to reduce dimensionality. We then used Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) ‐ an unsupervised statistical learning method originally designed to facilitate natural language processing ‐ to empirically generate k = 15 topics (similar groupings) of EMR elements. We described each patient's 24‐hour EMR record by 15 probability values ‐ one per topic, with values summing to 1. We added these topic distributions to a base logistic regression model for hospital mortality, which included age, gender, acute and chronic illness severity scores, direct ICU admission, and code status, to assess their added predictive value. Results. Overall, 45.6% of patients were male, mean age was 68 ± 17 years, and 6.2% died in the hospital. We identified 1,534,885 EMR elements, including 366,874 medications, 14,818 procedures, 955,921 orders, and 197,272 order sets. We excluded 1,300 elements occurring only once, leaving 3,001 unique elements. Adding topic composition to the base regression model improved the c‐statistic for mortality from 0.81 to 0.83 (p<0.01), explaining 25% of the variability. Compared to the lowest mortality reference topic (i.e., 'standard admission protocol'), all other topics were significantly associated with increased mortality (p<0.001). Table 1 presents selected topics, their mortality odds ratios (OR) per 10% increase, and the ten most frequent EMR elements comprising each topic. LDA modeling empirically identified clinically distinct EMR topics and reduced the effect estimates of highly significant predictors from the base model (e.g., direct ICU admission; OR: 1.66; p<0.001) to the augmented model (OR: 0.88; p=0.12). Conclusion. An unsupervised topic modeling approach incorporated 1.5 million EMR elements to statistically represent 30,000 infection hospitalizations as probability distributions of 15 clinically familiar topics. These empirical representations offer a novel machine learning approach to using highly‐granular EMR data to uncover latent clinical signatures among infection and sepsis patients. (Table Presented) .
