N2  -  Background and aims: The field of cognitive neurology is experiencing a technological revolution in recent years with the development of improved methods of detecting dementia. One such innovation is the Autonomous Cognitive Examination, which leverages the last 10–15 years advances in natural language processing and deep learning to enable robust and automated administration and assessment of an entire cognitive examination. This study presents preliminary results from a randomized controlled trial assessing the Autonomous Cognitive Examination. Methods: 13 subjects (six male, seven female), were randomly assigned to receive the Autonomous Cognitive Examination or Addenbrooke's Cognitive Examination‐3 first, then received the other 0–6 weeks later. Accuracy of the Autonomous Cognitive Examination was assessed via Spearman's Correlation, enabling demonstration of correlation of each cognitive domain, overall score, and allowed assessment of the machine learning methods performed. Results: We assessed overall performance with Spearman's Correlations for total score (r = 0.87, p = 0.00010), attention (r = 0.65, p = 0.016), fluency (r = 0.94, p = 0.00000), language (r = 0.72, p = 0.0052), memory (r = 0.93, p = 0.00041), and the visuospatial (r = 0.75, p = 0.0034). We also demonstrate assessment of the methods employed with Spearman's Correlations for computer vision (r = 1.00, p = 0.00000), natural language processing (r = 0.87, p = 0.00010), and non‐machine learning (r = 1.00, p = 0.00000). Conclusions: Overall, these findings provide strong support for the validity of the Autonomous Cognitive Examination and its software methods for assessing cognitive function. The Autonomous Cognitive Examination may offer several advantages over traditional paper‐based assessments, including increased efficiency, standardization, data collection, and scalability. Future research will include increasing the sample size and finalization of the clinical trial, then assessment of the Autonomous Cognitive Examination when deployed remotely.
