N2  -  Clinician training alone is often insufficient to increase adoption of evidence‐based psychotherapies (EBPs) as training does not address organizational barriers that may limit use of EBPs. To address such barriers, we developed a variant of external facilitation called Targeted Assessment and Context‐Tailored Implementation of Change Strategies (TACTICS). Staff in eight military treatment facilities received training in prolonged exposure therapy (PE) followed by TACTICS, with the timing of TACTICS randomized by site. TACTICS included designation of a local implementation team and an external coach, interviews to identify local barriers to PE use, developing an implementation plan, providing resources for supporting planned changes, and weekly coaching support for the site implementation champion. PE use was assessed through natural language processing of encounter note text in the health record. Controlling for time period, TACTICS increased use of PE relative to clinician training alone (OR CI = 1.05 – 2.21, p < .03). However, there was also a secular trend between the first and last time periods for declining use of PE (OR CI = .067 ‐ .316, p < .001). Post‐hoc analyses showed that the time effect on PE use was highly correlated with increases in workload and fewer available appointments per patient (r = ‐.98). Our results confirm that local‐level implementation support can increase adoption of EBPs relative to clinician training only. Still, there is a limit to what local implementation efforts can achieve unless they are accompanied by adequate staffing and by system‐level policies that incentivize use of EBPs.
