N2  -  Background: Several new psychological treatments for chronic pain are based on emerging affective neuroscience to target the emotional processes that trigger and maintain pain. Here, we present three studies that examined the outcomes, responder subgroups, and mediators of Intensive Short‐term Dynamic Psychology (ISTDP), Emotional Awareness and Expression Therapy (EAET), or Pain Reprocessing Therapy (PRT). Methods: Study 1 (Krohner) compared a 90‐minute ISTDP‐based interview to waitlist control on 1‐month pain outcomes for 92 people with chronic musculoskeletal pain and elevated childhood adversity. Study 2 (Guiffrida and Farah) conducted qualitative analyses (constructivist grounded theory) to understand which patients benefited the most from a 10‐week EAET group therapy. Study 3 (Ashar) used natural language processing to examine how changes in beliefs about the cause of chronic back pain predicted outcomes of PRT in an RCT. Results: The ISTDP‐based interview led to significant reductions in pain interference and pain anxiety and shifted attributions for pain from body to mind, compared to randomized waitlist controls. Study 2: Patients whose pain reduced substantially from EAET group therapy were more open to mindbody perspectives at baseline and more able to experience and express strong emotions than were patients who benefited less. Study 3: PRT vs. control led to large increases in mind/brain attributions, with increased mind/brain attributions correlating with larger pain reductions in the PRT condition at follow‐up. Conclusion: Discussant (Lumley) explores how these novel therapies substantially improve pain outcomes but could be enhanced by targeting treatment to appropriate patients and strengthening mind‐body attributions and emotional engagement.
