N2  -  Background: Suicide is a leading cause of death associated with psychiatric disorders. Existing suicide risk measures suffer from poor long‐term predictive value and risk assessment complexities are further exacerbated in telemedicine settings, where at‐distance evaluation can be a source of liability. To overcome these limitations, we analyzed linguistic markers of suicide using Natural Language Processing (NLP). Specifically, we designed a machine learning algorithm to detect suicide content from patients’ communication with their therapists, to support rapid clinical decision making. Methods: Transcripts of therapist‐patient dialogues were generated from telehealth therapy delivery. Clinical raters examined therapy vignettes and assessed suicide risk into five levels (no risk, risk factors only, ideation, method, plan). A labelled training dataset was produced, with heterogeneous levels of suicide risk. Patient transcripts were preprocessed into tf–idf features. A cross validated bag‐of‐words classification model was trained in Python (comparing performance of logistic regression, random forest, extremely randomized trees, XGBoost, multilayer perceptron), to detect patients’ level of suicide risk by analyzing their sentences. Results: The final NLP algorithm was trained on 1,864 patients (85,216 sentences). The best model detected patients’ level of suicide risk (no risk, risk factors only, ideation, method, plan) at the individual sentence level, with good accuracy (AUC=82.78). Conclusions: Suicide risk was reliably assessed using machine learning to analyze patients’ exchanges with their therapists. NLP offers a tool to analyze suicidal thoughts and behaviors, with important implications for rapid response in telemedicine settings. NLP could be further applied to mental health research, to identify computational linguistic markers of psychiatric disorders. Supported By: Talkspace Keywords: Suicide, Natural Language Processing (NLP), Digital Phenotyping, Computational Linguistics, Digital Health
