DO  -  10.1183/13993003.congress-2020.4570
N2  -  Background: Chronic cough (CC), cough lasting > 8 weeks, is difficult to identify due to coding inconsistencies. Objectives: We used unsupervised learning on ICD codes in the EHR to detect CC and investigate whether diagnoses can be used to reveal the relationships between CC and non‐CC cases. Methods: We identified adults with cough from 2005‐2015 using ICD diagnosis of cough, a positive instance of cough in the clinical notes per natural language processing, or a cough suppressant prescription. We further identified CC, or cough on at least 3 separate outpatient visits within a 120‐day window. We created 2 cohorts: (1) patients with CC (CC cohort) and (2) patients with cough, but not meeting the definition of CC (non‐CC cohort). We randomly selected 16,348 CC and 16,348 non‐CC patients with diagnosis, medication and lab data. Each patient was first represented as a vector i.e. an array of frequency of different ICD diagnoses within the 120‐day window. Normalization i.e. rescaling of the vector was done between 0 and 1. The K‐means clustering algorithm was used to partition data into CC and non‐CC clusters with k value for K‐means ranging from 2 to 30. The silhouette scores were calculated to determine the optimal k value. Results: At k=16, there are 2 clusters that have over 80% instances of CC (901 (CC)/161 (non‐CC)) and non‐CC (447(non‐CC)/55(CC)), respectively. Examination of feature significance of the clusters shows that CC predictors include respiratory, neoplastic, and other diseases, whereas non‐CC predictor includes patients with pregnancy complications. Conclusion: Multiple features predicted CC. Future work includes development of CC prediction algorithm.
