N2  -  Background Hypertension (HTN) is underrecognized, leading to preventable morbidity. This trial sought to determine whether previously collected cardiovascular imaging data could be harnessed to improve HTN detection and treatment through a system‐level intervention. Methods A two‐arm, randomized trial assessing the effect of a population health coordinator‐led notification system and clinical support pathway on antihypertensive prescribing. Natural language processing was developed to phenotype subjects. Eligible patients had left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH) on a prior echocardiogram, no known cardiomyopathy, and no prior antihypertensive prescriptions. Results 648 patients were randomized (mean age 59.4 years, 38% female). Over the 12‐month trial, 53 patients (16.3%) in the intervention arm were prescribed an antihypertensive medication versus 16 patients (5.0%) in the control arm (OR 3.76, p < 0.001), Figure. Intervention subjects were also more likely to be diagnosed with HTN (OR 4.43, p < 0.001). Notably, 71.9% of clinician responses to the trial notification were coded as having a positive sentiment. Conclusion A targeted notification and clinical support pathway for patients with LVH on prior echocardiograms significantly increased the initiation of HTN treatment. This work highlights the potential of leveraging preexisting but potentially underutilized data to improve cardiovascular health through mechanisms augmenting the traditional ambulatory care system. [Formula presented]
