DO  -  10.1111/eip.70046
N2  -  Psychotherapy training is critical to early intervention services but is notoriously difficult and costly to scale. There is a parallel deficiency in the psychotherapy training and psychotherapy delivery workforces. Furthermore, training practitioners to deliver psychotherapeutic interventions to fidelity requires years of training following baseline foundational and functional therapeutic competencies. Additionally, session‐level fidelity assessment is incompatible with the realities of most routine care settings across low‐, middle‐and high‐income countries and may fail to generalize to CBT adaptations, such as modularized, transdiagnostic, and digitally augmented CBT that are commonly found in early psychosis care settings. New approaches to provide standardized, cost‐effective, timely, and scalable performance‐based feedback of discrete psychotherapeutic competencies are now possible through the use of natural language processing and machine learning. This presentation will provide a rationale for exploring new ways of conceptualizing and operationalizing psychotherapy training and quality assurance in real world clinical settings that builds upon previous empirically‐evaluated approaches. In addition, the presentation will demonstrate the functionality of a newly developed Artificial Intelligence (AI)‐enhanced CBT fidelity tool, present machine learning validation data, and present data from this NIMH‐funded Research &amp; Development grant. Results suggest strong potential for responsibly‐developed AI to support automated training and clinical quality assurance across large systems of care. A multisite Randomized Controlled Trial, set to conclude in December 2024, will address research questions related to the tool's effectiveness in shaping practitioner behavior with real clients, client outcomes, and the facilitators and barriers to implementing asynchronous AI‐enabled training.
