DO  -  10.1111/jsr.14291
N2  -  Introduction: Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is a highly prevalent condition often diagnosed in elderly patients and/or with significant comorbidities that present special difficulty in initiating CPAP treatment. This results in poor therapy adherence and an overload on the healthcare system. AI, in constant development, emerges as a tool to optimize healthcare management. Hypothesis: the use of phone calls from a natural language processing‐based virtual assistant (TUCUVI) in the initiation of CPAP treatment for patients aged ≥65 is comparable in efficiency to conventional in‐person healthcare pathways. Main Objective: To compare the numbers of required visits, adherence and performing CPAP treatment. Method: Randomized non‐inferiority study of patients aged ≥65 with a recent diagnosis of OSA and eligible for CPAP treatment, comparing the usual in‐person follow‐up versus telephonic follow‐up with the virtual assistant TUCUVI over 6 months. Data on OSA diagnosis, symptoms, CPAP adherence, adverse effects and the number of inperson visits during follow‐up were collected. Adherence and compliance data at the 3‐month follow up are presented. At the end of the 6‐month follow up, a cost analysis, treatment adjustment assessment and satisfaction level evaluation will be performed. Results: 51 patients have been included, 26 in telephonic followup with TUCUVI and 25 in‐person, 56.9% male, with a mean age of 73.2 (SD 5.9) and an average AHI at diagnosis of 46.2/h (SD 16.3), with no differences between groups. Partial results from 30 patients who have completed 3 months of follow‐up show no significant differences between groups regarding adherence, compliance, persisting symptoms or additional in‐person visits. Conclusion: TUCUVI allows a correct initial adaptation to CPAP similar to conventional in‐person folloew‐up, with no significant issues and no additional in‐person visits required, based on a partial analysis at the 3 month follow up.
