N2  -  Introduction: While heterogeneity of asthma has been widely recognized and the application of AI to healthcare has been an emerging trend, little is known about whether a subfield of AI, natural language processing (NLP) algorithms for asthma phenotype at the time of onset predicts asthma prognosis. We assessed whether the application of validated NLP algorithms for two asthma criteria [predetermined asthma criteria (PAC) and asthma prediction index (API)] systematically identifies subgroups of asthmatic children with lower likelihood of achieving long‐term remission (ie, risk for persistent asthma). Methods: This was a retrospective birth cohort study which followed 1997‐2007 Olmsted County Birth Cohort up to 2017. Subjects meeting PAC (Predetermined asthma criteria) by our previously validated NLP algorithms (NLP‐PAC) were considered as having asthma. NLP‐API (Asthma Predictive Index) was further used to categorize subjects into two groups by atopy‐prone status: Group 1‐ considered atopic‐prone asthma (meeting both criteria), Group 2: less atopic‐prone asthma (meeting only PAC, not API). For outcome, a validated NLP algorithm for asthma prognosis was used to classify subjects into three groups (persistent asthma, long‐term remission, and intermittent remission) using asthma‐related events documented in the EHRs. Remission was defined as the absence of asthma events for at least 3 consecutive years after asthma onset. Socioeconomic status (SES) was measured by HOUSES index, an individual level real property based‐SES measure. Logistic regression model was used to determine the association between asthma phenotype by AI algorithms at the time of onset and asthma prognosis which was adjusted for age, frequency of well‐child visits and history of breastfeeding and childcare. Results: Among 570 subjects who met PAC definition, 56% were male, 82% were white. Mean age was 4 at asthma onset and 13 years at the last follow‐up date. 330 subjects (58%) also met API definition (Group 1) and 240 (42%) did not (Group 2). Compared to Group 2, Group 1, who met both PAC and API criteria had poor prognosis for having lower likelihood of achieving intermittent remission [Intermittent asthma [OR= 0.614 (0.365‐ 1.035), p=0.066] and long term remission [OR= 0.219 (0.133‐0.355), p<0.001] . Conclusion: AI (NLP) algorithms for asthma criteria helped to identify atopy‐prone asthma with poor asthma prognosis. AI can be considered a computational biomarker for asthma phenotype at the time of onset predicting asthma prognosis which may enable precision asthma care and research. The utility of these AI tools need to be tested through a Randomized clinical trial.
