DO  -  10.2139/ssrn.5220655
N2  -  Background: Mobile health (mHealth) systems often struggle to deliver personalized care underpinned by a robust theoretical framework for type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) management. To address these challenges, we developed the artificial intelligence‐based health education accurately linking system (AI‐HEALS), which integrates a T2DM knowledge graph with natural language processing to offer tailored health education via the WeChat platform. Methods: In this single‐blind, cluster‐randomized controlled trial (July 2023–July 2024), 391 adults with T2DM, aged 18–75 years, were recruited from 45 community health centers (CHCs) in Beijing, China, and randomly allocated (1:1) to the AI‐HEALS intervention (n=202) or treatment‐as‐usual (TAU; n=189). CHCs clinicians, field investigators, and data analysts were masked to allocation. The primary outcome was changed in HbA1c at 3, 6, and 12 months, and secondary outcomes included self‐management behaviors (self‐care, dietary adherence, medication adherence, diabetes skills), sleep quality (B‐PSQI), perceived stress (PSS‐4), self‐efficacy, and quality of life (VAS). This trial is registered with the Chinese Clinical Trial Registry (ChiCTR2300068952). Findings: Baseline HbA1c was similar between groups (AI: 7.29% (95% CI 7.13 to 7.46) versus TAU: 7.42% (95% CI 7.24 to 7.59); P = 0.294). At 6 months, patients receiving the AI‐HEALS intervention demonstrated a significant HbA1c reduction (difference, −0.23% (95% CI −0.46 to −0.00); P = 0.049). Although the between‐group difference narrowed by 12 months, with the AI group achieving a mean reduction of −0.31% (95% CI −0.51 to −0.11) compared to −0.23% (95% CI −0.44 to −0.02) in the TAU group (P = 0.523), more AI participants reached an HbA1c ＜7.0%. In addition, the AI‐HEALS intervention resulted in marked improvements in self‐care, dietary adherence, diabetes‐related skills, sleep quality, perceived stress, self‐efficacy, and quality of life. No serious adverse events were observed, underscoring the safety and effectiveness of this approach. Interpretation: At 6 months, AI‐HEALS significantly improved glycemic control. Although the difference in HbA1c compared with the TAU group was attenuated at 12 months, the AI arm still achieved a greater reduction in HbA1c and markedly enhanced self‐management, particularly in dietary management. The system offers a theory‐driven, scalable mHealth solution for T2DM management in resource‐limited settings.
