DO  -  10.1111/acem.13756
N2  -  Background: Chest pain is the second most frequent reason for all U.S. emergency department (ED) visits. Non‐invasive cardiac tests including electrocardiogram (ECG) treadmill stress tests (ETT) are recommended for evaluation of suspected acute coronary syndrome (ACS) for patients who present to the ED. However, recent evidence questions the effectiveness of these non‐invasive tests and the resources required to review thousands of charts limits the ability to include ETT results in large studies. Therefore, the goal of this study was to develop an algorithm to identify ETT results using natural language processing (NLP). Methods: An NLP algorithm was developed to search unstructured ETT reports to identify results demonstrating ischemia in a diverse cohort of ED patients (n=5533). A set of randomly selected reports were double‐blind reviewed by two physicians to create the training (n=100) and validation (n=105) data. ETT reports were searched for diagnostic statements as well as a set of variables (e.g. ECG, symptoms, heart rate (HR), blood pressure). The final ETT results were derived based on the information extracted from the reports. Each NLP component was validated against the validation data. Additional variables (e.g. chronotropic index, hypertensive response) were also created using the information extracted. Results: NLP achieved 96.4% sensitivity, 94.8% specificity in identifying non‐normal (abnormal/equivocal/non‐diagnostic) vs. normal ETT tests. Of the 5533 ETT reports, 25.4% had a non‐normal test results (abnormal 5.7%, equivocal/non‐diagnostic 19.7%). Metabolic equivalent was identified in 97.2% reports (median, Q1, Q3: 10.1, 7.2, 11.2). Maximum HR achieved was identified in 88.6% reports (median, Q1, Q3: 92.0%, 87%, 98%). Exercise time was identified in 86.6% reports (median, Q1, Q3: 8.0, 6.3, 9.7 minutes). Hypertensive responses were identified in 27.2% of tests. Chronotropic incompetence (chronotropic index < 0.8) was identified in 31.4% of tests. Conclusion: Our results suggest that NLP can be used to identify clinically meaningful information documented in the treadmill stress tests reports. The automated identification of treadmill stress tests results and associated variables facilitates research studies to evaluate the appropriate care strategies for patients who present with suspected ACS in the ED settings.
