N2  -  Introduction: Goals‐of‐care discussions are important for high‐quality palliative care yet are often lacking for hospitalized older patients with serious illness. We sought to evaluate a communication‐priming intervention to promote goals‐of‐care discussions for these patients. Methods: We conducted a pragmatic, parallel, 1:1 randomized trial of a communication‐priming intervention compared to usual care at three hospitals in one healthcare system, including a university, county, and community hospital. Eligible hospitalized patients were over age 55 with a chronic, life‐limiting illness or over age 80. We excluded patients with a documented goals‐of‐care discussion or palliative care consult prior to randomization. We screened 3918 consecutive patients to identify 2512 eligible patients. Inpatient physicians and advance practice providers of patients randomized to the intervention received a one‐page, patient‐specific Jumpstart Guide to prompt and guide a goals‐of‐care discussion. All data were from electronic health records (EHR). The primary outcome was proportion of patients with a natural language processing (NLP)‐ identified goals‐of‐care discussion within 30 days of randomization. Secondary outcomes included intensity of care within 30 days of randomization. We examined heterogeneity of treatment effects for the primary outcome by patient age, gender, minoritized race or ethnicity, and dementia. Results: 2512 patients (mean age, 71.7 years; 42% women; 13% Black, 12% Asian, 2% Native American or Pacific Islander, 70% White; 93% non‐Hispanic, 6% Hispanic) were randomized (1255 to intervention, 1257 to usual care). The proportion of patients with goals‐of‐care discussions was 34.0% with intervention and 29.9% with usual care for a difference of 4.1% (95% CI 0.4‐7.8). Significant effects of intervention on secondary outcomes were not detected. Among 803 patients whose race was not White or whose ethnicity was Hispanic, the proportion of goals‐of‐care discussions was 10.2% higher with intervention compared to usual care (33.5 vs 23.3%), while among 1,641 non‐Hispanic Whites, it was 1.6% higher (35.3 vs. 33.7%; p=0.03 for interaction). Differential effects of intervention on the primary outcome by other characteristics were not detected. Conclusions: Jumpstart resulted in a small but statistically significant increase in goals‐of‐care discussions. Assessment of heterogeneity of treatment effects suggests Jumpstart may be more effective for racially‐ or ethnically‐minoritized individuals. (Figure Presented).
