N2  -  Aim: (1) To examine feasibility and acceptability of an innovative short‐term Virtual Reality (VR) based treatment for negative symptoms in patients with schizophrenia‐spectrum disorders (SSD); (2) to assess the preliminary efficacy of the treatment in alleviating negative symptoms, improving daily life functioning and altering the reward processing system. Method: In this clinical pilot trial, 30 patients are being recruited from Copenhagen Mental Health Services' outpatient units for early intervention in psychosis. Participants are randomised to receive either treatment‐as‐ usual (TAU) or an additional 10 sessions of VR based psychotherapy targeting social reward learning. As primary outcome, feasibility and acceptability are operationalised as recruitment of = 80% of the target sample within 15 months, = 70% retention to study protocol at cessation of therapy (10 sessions), and = 80% reporting a satisfaction rating of = 7 on a 10‐point Likert scale. Treatment effect will be evaluated by comparing patients' symptom levels and level of functioning as assessed by a rater and by Ecological Momentary Assessment at baseline and after treatment (3 months). As explorative outcomes, structural and functional MRI and free speech, employing Natural Language Processing, will be analysed pre‐and post‐treatment. Results: Patient enrolment has been ongoing since November 2024. Preliminary results on feasibility, acceptability, and treatment effect will be presented at the conference. Conclusion: A novel psychological treatment for negative symptoms in SSD is currently being piloted. If feasible and acceptable, an appropriately powered RCT will investigate the intervention's efficacy, aiming to expand the current treatment options for negative symptoms.
