N2  -  Background: In patients with colorectal cancer metastases confined to the liver (CRLM), resection of the primary tumor and liver metastases may offer a chance of cure or long‐term survival. Most patients however present with unresectable CRLM. Hepatic arterial infusion pump (HAIP) therapy combined with systemic therapy (HAIP‐SYST) could enhance conversion to resectable CRLM, reduce recurrences and prolong survival. The aim of this trial is to investigate whether intensifying induction treatment with HAIP‐SYST therapy prolongs survival in chemotherapy‐naive patients with initially unresectable CRLM as compared to systemic therapy alone. Material and methods: This multicenter, phase 3 randomized controlled trial will be conducted in all Dutch liver centers and 306 patients will be included. Eligible trial participants are chemotherapy‐naive patients with synchronous unresectable CRLM without extrahepatic disease and with suitable hepatic arterial anatomy for potential pump placement. Assessment of (un)resectability status is done by a national liver panel (NLP) based on predefined criteria. Patients randomized to arm A receive induction systemic therapy according to standard clinical practice. Patients in arm B will are treated in one of the specialized pump centers and undergo surgery for pump placement and resection of the primary tumor before start of HAIP‐SYST. Floxuridine is the chemotherapeutic agent used for hepatic arterial infusion. Treatment response and conversion to local treatment of CRLM is evaluated by the NLP every two months during induction treatment. All patients are followed for five years from date of randomization or death. Primary endpoint is overall survival (OS), defined as the time between randomization and the event of death. Secondary endpoints include progression‐free survival (PFS), hepatic PFS, conversion‐to‐resection rates, R0/R1 resection rates, radiological and pathological response, surgical complications, toxicity, quality of life and cost‐effectiveness. Results (For Surgical Trial Proposals fill in ‘the Feasibility’, for Surgical Trial in Progress fill in the ‘Current status’): The PUMP‐IT trial will open for accrual in summer 2024. A total of 306 patients will be included and planned accrual is 5.5 years. Analysis of the primary endpoint is expected after 6 years. The trial is registered at European Clinical Trials Database (EudraCT): 2023‐506194‐35‐00. Conclusions (For Surgical Trial in Progress and Surgical Trial Proposals fill in ‘NA’): NA
