N2  -  Background and Aims: Integration of a chatbot in a pre‐anaesthetic clinic (PAC) could improve efficiency, convenience, and data collection. We evaluated the feasibility and acceptability of chatbot‐assisted PAC. Methods: Ethics approval (ECASM‐AIMS‐2024‐281) and Clinical Trials Registry‐India registration (CTRI/2024/11/076403) were obtained. The entire PAC questions were algorithmised, integrated to chatbot, and made accessible via QR code. Consenting patients 18–60 years scheduled for elective abdominal surgery, who had access to smartphones, and who were comfortable in English were included. Others were excluded. Patients were randomised into two groups: Group C (chatbot PAC followed by anaesthestist PAC) and Group H (anaesthestist PAC followed by chatbot PAC). Anaesthetists and patients were blinded to allocation sequence, and the time taken for PAC was recorded. Anonymised chatbot transcripts were summarised to 250 words by prompt engineering using five Large Language Models (ChatGPT, Perplexity, Llama, Grok, DeepSeek). The summaries were evaluated for lexical overlap (ROUGE Score) and semantic similarity (BERT Score) using Python and also manually using a validated questionnaire.(Figure Presented) Results: A total of 212 patients were recruited (sample size determined via pilot study to show 20% reduction in PAC time with 80% power and 95% confidence). Chatbot PAC saved 20.4% time (15.15+3.73 v/s 19.04+3.77minutes, p < 0.0001). Over 98% anaesthetists reported satisfaction, citing efficiency and documentation ease. 5% patients preferred anaesthestist PAC. Llama produced the best summaries. Conclusion: Chatbot‐assisted Llama‐summarised PAC reduced consultation time, improved workflow efficiency, and was highly acceptable to patients and clinicians. Future work should expand chatbot functions to airway assessment, assist differently abled, and provide multilingual options.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-03023089/full
