N2  -  Introduction &amp; Objectives: In India, adolescent males often lack structured sexual‐health education due to cultural stigma, misinformation, and limited access to professional counseling. These barriers contribute to preventable reproductive morbidity, sexually transmitted infections (STIs), and psychosocial issues. AI‐driven conversational agents can provide anonymous, evidence‐based sexual‐health information at scale. We developed a WhatsApp‐integrated chatbot using natural language processing (NLP) to deliver personalized, WHO‐aligned educational content to adolescent males in Mumbai. This randomized controlled trial evaluated its effectiveness in improving sexual‐health knowledge and behavioral intent compared to traditional educational methods. Materials &amp; Methods: A single‐center, parallel‐group randomized controlled trial was conducted at JJ Hospital, Mumbai, between January and August 2025. Participants: 300 adolescent males aged 14–18 years were recruited from outpatient and community outreach programs. Intervention: AI chatbot (Rasa/Dialogflow architecture) delivering interactive modules on puberty, contraception, STI prevention, consent, and relationships. Control: Standard printed educational booklet with one‐time counseling. Primary Outcome: Change in a validated 20‐item sexual‐health knowledge score from baseline to 3 months. Secondary Outcomes: Behavioral intent indices (condom‐use intent, STI testing awareness), user engagement metrics (session frequency, duration), and satisfaction (Net Promoter Score). Data were analyzed using paired t‐tests for within‐group change and ANCOVA for between‐group differences (p < 0.05 considered significant). Ethical approval was obtained from the Institutional Ethics Committee, and both parental consent and participant assent were secured. Results: The study was completed in August 2025 with 300 participants (150 per arm). Knowledge Improvement: Mean knowledge score increased by 34% ± 10 in the chatbot group vs 13% ± 9 in controls (p < 0.001). Behavioral Outcomes: Condom‐use intent improved from 39% to 68% in the intervention arm vs 41% to 53% in controls (p = 0.02). Engagement: Average 4.3 sessions/week with 82% 3‐month retention. Satisfaction: Net Promoter Score +61 in intervention vs +27 in control. No adverse ethical or privacy incidents occurred. Conclusions: An AI‐based, WhatsApp‐integrated chatbot significantly improved sexual‐health literacy and positive behavioral intent among adolescent males compared with standard educational methods. This low‐cost, privacy‐preserving digital intervention demonstrates strong potential for scalable deployment in resource‐limited settings. Integrating AI‐driven education into public‐health programs may help reduce future urological and reproductive morbidities while promoting equitable access to reliable sexual‐health information.
