N2  -  INTERVENTION: Group 1: Intervention group – Discharge consultation supported by AI‐generated layperson‐friendly discharge letter Participants in this group receive, prior to the discharge consultation, both the original medical discharge letter and an AI‐generated simplified version of the same document. The layperson‐friendly translation is created by a natural language processing tool designed to enhance patient understanding. The intervention aims to improve patient comprehension, communication quality, and satisfaction with the discharge process. Group 2: Control group – Discharge consultation with standard medical discharge letter, no AI support Participants in this group receive only the standard medical discharge letter prior to the discharge consultation. No additional AI‐based simplification or language adaptation is provided. The consultation and communication are conducted according to standard clinical procedures. CONDITION: The study includes patients with a wide range of neurosurgical conditions. These include, but are not limited to, degenerative spinal diseases (e.g., disc herniations, spinal stenosis), intracranial space‐occupying lesions (e.g., gliomas, meningiomas), cerebrovascular disorders (e.g., aneurysms, hemorrhages, ischemic strokes), epilepsy, hydrocephalus, and congenital malformations. The inclusion is independent of the specific diagnosis, as the focus of the study lies on medical document comprehensibility and doctor‐patient communication. PRIMARY OUTCOME: Patient satisfaction with physician communication during the discharge consultation; ; Patient satisfaction is assessed immediately after the discharge consultation using the Health Care Communication Questionnaire (HCCQ) and the Patient‐Physician Interaction Questionnaire (FAPI). The study compares the intervention and control groups regarding perceived communication quality and clarity of information. SECONDARY OUTCOME: Self‐perceived understanding, consultation duration, physician satisfaction ; ; Secondary outcomes include: ; ‐ Patients’ self‐assessed understanding of the discharge letter using a 1–5 numeric scale ; ‐ Duration of the discharge consultation, recorded by time documentation (start–end) ; ‐ Physician satisfaction with the consultation, assessed via the HCCQ (physician version) and Difficult Doctor‐Patient Relationship Questionnaire (DDPRQ) INCLUSION CRITERIA: ‐ Inpatient treatment at the Department of Neurosurgery with scheduled discharge ‐ Planned physician‐led discharge consultation including handover of the discharge letter ‐ Sufficient German language proficiency to complete the survey ‐ Cognitive ability to self‐assess understanding ‐ Written informed consent provided
